@@ -7,7 +7,7 @@ Plan task T56, parts 1 (#2037), 2 (#2025), the hand-back check (#2116), the pre-
 1. **Task:** `IssueReads::listOpen(<project>, null)` (the GitHub forge's contract) lists the open issues and the run takes the one numbered `<issue>`. The caller names the issue, so the eligibility and order rules of intake do not apply. A pull request of that number is no task; a forge read that fails (`ForgeReadFailed`, `ForgeRateLimited`) or an issue the listing lacks ends the run here. The result holds the `ListedIssue` (title and body, untrusted text) for the steps that come later.
 2. **Mirror:** `BuildCheckout::updateMirror(<project>, <remote>)`, the Git module's contract for [`intakt:mirror:update`](mirror.md).
 3. **Clone:** `BuildCheckout::makeClone(<project>, <branch>, <folder>)`, the contract for [`intakt:build:clone`](build-clone.md), without a source line (whether the task's author may name one is not read here).
-4. **Build job:** `RunBuildJob::run(BuildJob)`, the use case of [`intakt:build:run`](build-job.md), in `<folder>` on `<branch>` with `<image>`. The tier is the routing rules' choice: `BuildRouting::tierFor(<project>, BuildFacts::fromLabels(<the issue's labels>, []))`, so a `model:<tier>` label of the issue counts, with no paths and no setback (a first build has no file forecast and no earlier round). The brief is the issue's title, a blank line and its body, with no decisions and no rework; `BuilderPrompt` fences it. The step passes when the job hands back a bundle and the pull request text (`BuildVerdict::Built`). A job that ends with a question that passes the question rule (`BuildVerdict::Asked`, with its `BuildQuestion`) parks the task: `run()` calls `ParkedTasks::park(<project>, <issue>, <question>)`, which records this step as `asked` (see [The parked-task record](#the-parked-task-record)), and the run ends there without a failure and runs no later step; a parking that fails (`TaskNotParked`) fails the step with that exception's fixed words. A question that breaks the rule (`BuildVerdict::QuestionRefused`) and any other verdict fail it with `BuildVerdict::reason()` as its message (for example `the build made no commit on the task's branch`). A project without routing rules (`BuildRoutingNotConfigured`), a tier that does not resolve or is not on `claude_code` (`TierResolutionFailure`, `BuilderNotOnClaudeCode`), a clone that is no folder, a job the runner refuses (`JobRefused`), a failing engine (`DockerEngineFailed`), a job event the ledger did not record (`JobEventNotRecorded`) and a filesystem error fail it with their message. What the job hands back (the bundle, the pull request text and the log) is untrusted: only the verdict reaches the ledger; the bundle goes on to the hand-back check and the pre-push check, the pull request text to the push step, and the log nowhere.
+4. **Build job:** `RunBuildJob::run(BuildJob)`, the use case of [`intakt:build:run`](build-job.md), in `<folder>` on `<branch>` with `<image>`. The tier is the routing rules' choice: `BuildRouting::tierFor(<project>, BuildFacts::fromLabels(<the issue's labels>, []))`, so a `model:<tier>` label of the issue counts, with no paths, no setback and no escalation outcome (a first build has no file forecast and no earlier round). The brief is the issue's title, a blank line and its body, with no decisions and no rework; `BuilderPrompt` fences it. The step passes when the job hands back a bundle and the pull request text (`BuildVerdict::Built`). A job that ends with a question that passes the question rule (`BuildVerdict::Asked`, with its `BuildQuestion`) parks the task: `run()` calls `ParkedTasks::park(<project>, <issue>, <question>)`, which records this step as `asked` (see [The parked-task record](#the-parked-task-record)), and the run ends there without a failure and runs no later step; a parking that fails (`TaskNotParked`) fails the step with that exception's fixed words. A question that breaks the rule (`BuildVerdict::QuestionRefused`) and any other verdict fail it with `BuildVerdict::reason()` as its message (for example `the build made no commit on the task's branch`). A project without routing rules (`BuildRoutingNotConfigured`), a tier that does not resolve or is not on `claude_code` (`TierResolutionFailure`, `BuilderNotOnClaudeCode`), a clone that is no folder, a job the runner refuses (`JobRefused`), a failing engine (`DockerEngineFailed`), a job event the ledger did not record (`JobEventNotRecorded`) and a filesystem error fail it with their message. What the job hands back (the bundle, the pull request text and the log) is untrusted: only the verdict reaches the ledger; the bundle goes on to the hand-back check and the pre-push check, the pull request text to the push step, and the log nowhere.
 5. **Hand-back check:** `UseCase/BuildTask/HandBackCheck` has `UseCase/BuildTask/HandBackFile` write the bundle to a file of its own (`Filesystem::tempnam()` in `var/hand-backs`, made with mode `0700`), calls `HandBackChecks::check(HandBack)`, the HandBack module's contract for the check of [`intakt:hand-back:check`](hand-back.md) (implemented by `CheckHandBack`), and `HandBackFile` removes the file after the check, whatever its result, also when writing the bundle into it failed. The `HandBack` names the project's mirror (`BuildCheckout::mirrorOf(<project>)`), because the check needs a bare repository whose `HEAD` names main and the clone's `HEAD` names the task's branch; `<branch>`; the file; no merge of main asked (a first build has no rework that asks for one); and an empty `JobCredentials`, because the build job mounts no credential yet. An `AcceptedHandBack` leaves its objects in the mirror, named by no ref, and then `BuildCheckout::takeCheckedHead(<project>, BranchHead(<branch>, <head>), <folder>)` moves the clone's branch to the accepted head, because the job committed in a clone of its own in the container, so `<folder>` holds the checked build only after this. The step passes once the clone took the head, and `HandBackCheck::check()` then returns the `AcceptedHandBack` (its `base` and `head`) for the push step instead of a failure text. A `RefusedHandBack` fails it with the refusal's fixed text (`HandBackRefusal::reason()`, for example `the bundle cannot be read as a git bundle`), so no text of the bundle reaches the ledger. A check that cannot run (`HandBackNotChecked`, for example when gitleaks is missing), an invalid branch (`HandBackBranchRefused`), a head that is no object id (`InvalidArgumentException` of `BranchHead`), a refused attributes file (`RepositoryAttributesRefused`), a project name that is no folder name (`BuildCheckoutFailed`), a bundle file that cannot be written (`IOException`) and a clone that cannot take the head (`BuildCheckoutFailed`) fail it with their message. A bundle file that cannot be removed after the check (`IOException` of `Filesystem::remove()`) fails the step with its message, which names the file's path and nothing of the bundle, in place of the check's own result: also after an accepted bundle whose head the clone already took, because the untrusted bundle is then still on disk. A failure of Symfony Process during the check (git or gitleaks killed by a signal, past its timeout, or unable to run) fails it with fixed words (`the hand-back check was killed by signal <n>`, `the hand-back check timed out after <n> seconds`, `the hand-back check could not run`), never with the exception's message, which holds the whole command line. The pull request text is not checked here, and a failure is not sent back to the builder (that is the round of `RouteHandBack`, which this step does not run).
 6. **Pre-push check:** `BuildTask` first calls `BuildCheckout::updateMirror(<project>, <remote>)` again, as the mirror step does, because main can move while the build job runs and the check job merges the branch into current main (the mirror's `HEAD`); a failed update fails this step with its message and starts no check job. Then `UseCase/BuildTask/PrePushCheck` has `HandBackFile` write the same bundle to a new file and build the same `HandBack`, then calls `CheckJobs::run(CheckJob)`, the HandBack module's contract for the [check job](check-job.md) of `intakt:check:run` (implemented by `RunCheckJob`), with `<image>`, no configured command (so the gate's own steps run: `composer install` and `bin/check --fast` for the PHP gate, with an empty Composer cache, because no check uses the gate's [dependency step](check-job.md#the-gate-plugin) yet) and no path named by the plan, because neither a project's check command nor plan approval is configured yet. `RunCheckJob` runs `CheckHandBack` again on the same bundle and the updated mirror. The hand-back check moves no ref of the mirror, but neither it nor `RunCheckJob` holds the mirror's lock (`UpdateMirror` takes `git-mirror-<project>` only to publish a new mirror), so another operation on the same mirror, for example another run's update of the project's mirror, can move its refs between the two checks. Apart from the update this step itself runs first (its effect is described next), the second check always judges the bundle again against the mirror as it then is (main as its `HEAD` names it and the task's branch as it then stands), so it can refuse a bundle the first check accepted; any refusal fails the step as below. When the update fetched the task's branch (a concurrent push since the mirror step) and that fetched branch is not an ancestor of the bundle's head (the push holds other commits), the check refuses the bundle with `HandBackRefusal::NotFastForward`, and the step fails with that refusal's fixed text and starts no check job. When the fetched branch is an ancestor of the bundle's head (for example a branch created on the remote at the main commit the build started from), the bundle still passes and the check job runs. After a passed check come the lost-content check and the check job, which clones the mirror, applies the bundle itself and, when main moved since the branch last merged it, runs the steps on the branch merged into current main, as CI does. The step passes when the outcome is `CheckVerdict::Passed`. A merge with current main that conflicts is the conflict route: `CheckVerdict::ConflictsWithMain` fails the step with `the branch conflicts with current main, so the check ran no step`, the conflicting paths stay in the check's log, and nothing is pushed, as with the push step's conflict below. A merge that fails without git reporting a conflict (git refused it, its commit failed or it left no merge commit) is `CheckVerdict::NotMerged` and fails the step with `git refused to merge the branch with current main without a conflict, so the check ran no step`. Any other verdict fails it with `CheckVerdict::reason()`, with ` (exit code <n>)` for a failed step (for example `the check command failed (exit code 2)`); the check's log never reaches the ledger or the output, because it holds what the code under test printed. A `RefusedHandBack` fails it with the refusal's fixed text. A check that cannot run (`HandBackNotChecked`), an invalid branch (`HandBackBranchRefused`), a refused attributes file (`RepositoryAttributesRefused`), a job the runner refuses (`JobRefused`), a failing engine (`DockerEngineFailed`), a job event the ledger did not record (`JobEventNotRecorded`), a project name that is no folder name (`BuildCheckoutFailed`) and a bundle file or check script that cannot be written or read (`IOException`) fail it with their message; a bundle file that cannot be removed after the check fails it as in the hand-back check. A failure of Symfony Process during the check fails it with fixed words (`the pre-push check was killed by signal <n>`, `the pre-push check timed out after <n> seconds`, `the pre-push check could not run`). After a passed pre-push check, `<folder>` holds the checked head on `<branch>`, ready for the push step. A failure is not retried and not sent back to the builder.
 7. **Push:** the signed push of the accepted head, described in the next section.
