 Routing rules in the configuration pick the tier a build starts on. This page covers the rules and their reader (parts 1 and 2 of the model-choice task, T60: the default, label and sensitive path rules, and raising the tier after a setback) and part 3 (#1748), which turns the tier into a model and records the choice as a `model_chosen` ledger event.
 
-**Facts.** `Core\Build\Contract\BuildFacts` holds the facts of a build: the tier a `model:` label names (`labelledTier`, null without one), the paths the build touches and the `Contract\Setback` of the earlier round (`setback`, null without one). `BuildFacts::fromLabels($labels, $paths)` reads a `model:<tier>` label with `Tier::tryFrom`, so a value that names a model or a vendor is ignored, and the last valid label wins. `Setback` has `failed-attempt` (an earlier attempt failed), `blocking-finding` (the round before had a P1) and `stall`; a round without a P1 is no setback, so the caller passes null. The loop passes it from what it knows, never from builder output. The class has no field for anything the builder says, so a job cannot choose its own model; a test pins its fields.
+**Facts.** `Core\Build\Contract\BuildFacts` holds the facts of a build: the tier a `model:` label names (`labelledTier`, null without one), the paths the build touches and the `Contract\Setback` of the earlier round (`setback`, null without one). `BuildFacts::fromLabels($labels, $paths)` reads a `model:<tier>` label with `Tier::tryFrom`, so a value that names a model or a vendor is ignored, and the last valid label wins. `Setback` has `failed-attempt` (an earlier attempt failed), `blocking-finding` (the round before had a P1) and `stall`; a round without a P1 is no setback, so the caller passes null. `escalation` holds the `Review\Contract\EscalationOutcome` of the review rounds on the head of the change's latest round (null without one), set by `BuildFacts::afterEscalation()`. The loop passes these from what it knows, never from builder output. The class has no field for anything the builder says, so a job cannot choose its own model; a test pins its fields.
 
