+## An escalation review
+
+The part of T61 that asks for and acts on the verdict (issue #1759). `UseCase\EscalateRecurringP1\EscalateRecurringP1Handler` (an `AsCommandHandler`) takes one `EscalateRecurringP1` from the queue:
+
+- **One at a time per key:** it takes a Symfony Lock on the key (`review-escalation-` and the key's SHA-256) without waiting. When another delivery holds it, it throws `EscalationBusy`, a `RecoverableMessageHandlingException`, so Messenger brings the message back after the retry strategy's delay and it then reuses the verdict the other delivery kept: two deliveries of one key never pay for two answers.
+- **Once per round and key:** under the lock it first reads what the ledger holds for the round and key (`EscalationsOfChange::recordedFor()`: `escalation_reviewed`, `escalation_verdict_reused` or `escalation_failed`). When one is there, a redelivery records nothing and asks no model, a failure included. Otherwise a verdict kept by another round (`KeptVerdicts::ofKey()`) is reused (`escalation_verdict_reused`, as in [A kept escalation verdict](#a-kept-escalation-verdict)), so a key never gets a second, possibly contradicting verdict.
+- **Refused before the call:** `EscalationRequests::of()` refuses a finding the round did not report (`StoredFindings::reportedIn()`) and a finding whose files are not in the diff or whose part of the diff is longer than the prompt holds (`EscalationPrompt::showsTheFindingWhole()`, `PromptCut::CEILING_BYTES`), and a project that maps no model to the strong tier (`Vendor::mapsAModelTo()` with `Plugin/Vendor/Contract/EscalationReviews::TIER`, `Tier::Strong`) is refused too; each appends `escalation_failed` (`Contract\EscalationFailed`: `round`, `finding`, `key`, `reason`, Intakt's own words) without a model call.
+- **The call:** `EscalationReviews::escalate(project, EscalationRequest)` gets the round's task and diff and that one finding (`Contract\EscalationRequest`), nothing else of the change (see [Vendor](vendor.md#escalation-review)). `EscalationPrompt` puts the sections of the files the finding names first in the diff (split at each `diff --git ` header, paths read by `DiffPaths`), then the others in their order, so the cut keeps the finding's hunks. An answer that is no escalation verdict comes back as null and appends `escalation_failed` with `the answer could not be read as an escalation verdict`; no verdict is kept, so a failed escalation never counts as rejected.
+- **The files the finding names:** the same names its key was built from, `FindingKeys::citedIn()` (the `file` field and every citation in the text, a name without a directory standing for the diff path that carries it), so a P1 whose `file` field is null or a bare name still gets its files first; when none of them is in the diff, or their sections are longer than the prompt holds, the request is refused with `EscalationRequests::FINDING_CUT` as above.
+- **A call that keeps failing:** `ProviderRateLimited` and `VendorAccountBusy` become `EscalationLimited::untilFree()`, a `RecoverableMessageHandlingException` with `forceRetry` on and the rate limit's reset as its delay (none for a busy account, so the transport's `max_delay` caps the wait), so they are waited out and never parked; only `ProviderFailedTransiently` becomes `EscalationLimited::bounded()`, with `forceRetry` off, so the `async` transport's `max_retries` bound it; `RecordRecurringP1s` queues each `EscalateRecurringP1` with the `DispatchOnFailureStamp` of `RecordFailedEscalation`, so a message parked for good (for any error) queues that record, and `RecordFailedEscalationHandler`, under the key's lock and once per round and key, appends `escalation_failed` with `RecordFailedEscalationHandler::PARKED` and logs an error, so the change is no longer pending.
+- **Keeping it:** the reasons are redacted (`Redactor`), and under the round's lock and in one transaction `KeptVerdicts::keep()` stores the verdict and `RoundLedger::escalationReviewed()` appends `escalation_reviewed` (`Contract\EscalationReviewed`: `round`, `finding`, `key`, `outcome`, `reasons`). When another writer kept the key during the call (`VerdictAlreadyKept`), its verdict is reused instead.
+
+The outcomes are read by plain code from the ledger and the kept verdicts (`UseCase\EscalateRecurringP1\EscalationsOfChange`), never from model text:
+
+- **Confirmed:** `Contract\ChangeEscalations::outcomeAtLatestHead(project, change)` reads the rounds of the change on the head of its latest round (`ReviewRounds::latestOfChange()`), so a rejection's re-review on the same head does not hide a confirmation; `confirmed` wins when several findings were escalated, and a round on a newer head that has not been escalated has none. While a `recurring_p1_found` of those rounds has no `escalation_reviewed`, `escalation_verdict_reused` or `escalation_failed` for its key, the escalation is still queued and it throws `Contract\EscalationStillPending`, so no build tier is chosen before the answer. The build's tier commands read it with `--change` and route the next build to the strong tier (see [Model choice](model-choice.md)). The finding stays open, so the panel whose reviewer raised it reviews the next round with the confirmation.
+- **Rejected:** `HolderReReviews::startAfterRejectionIn()` dispatches `Contract\StartReviewAtHead` for the next round of the change (number + 1) on the rejected round's head, from the repository and base of its `review_started`, so the holder re-reviews before a build is asked to fix the finding. It does so after a fresh rejection, on a redelivery and on a rejection reused at the judging (for which `RecordRecurringP1s` queues `EscalateRecurringP1` too); a start refused as `RoundNotNext` (another rejection or a newer round started it) starts nothing. It starts no round for a round without a source (no repository to read the change from) and none when an earlier round of the change is on the same head, because that round's holder has re-reviewed with the reasons already; holding the finding again is its answer. The finding stays open with the reviewer that holds it, and the next round's `ReviewBrief::$escalations` carries the kept verdict of each open finding an earlier round escalated, by finding id (`EscalationsOfChange::ofOpenFindings()`, the latest escalation of each finding winning). `ReviewWithReviewerHandler` fills it, and `ReviewerPrompt` adds one user message after the open findings with the outcome and reasons of each, fenced as `escalation verdicts`, asking the reviewer to hold a rejected finding again only when the reasons are wrong for the code it reads. A resolved finding carries no verdict into the next round.
+
