-A build that touches a sensitive path starts on the strong tier, whatever its label says. Otherwise, after a listed setback (an earlier failed attempt, a blocking P1 finding or a stall) the build is raised to the strong tier; a rework after a round without a P1 stays on the cheaper tier. Otherwise a `model:<tier>` label (for example `model:strong`) sets the tier, and without either the build starts on `tier`. A `model:` value that names no tier, such as a model name, is ignored. The rules look only at the labels, the touched paths and the setback the loop passes, so a job can never choose its own model. A bad tier, an unknown setback, or sensitive paths or setbacks that are not a list, stop Intakt from starting and name the key.
+A build that touches a sensitive path starts on the strong tier, whatever its label says. Otherwise, when a review round on the latest reviewed head of the change it continues confirmed a P1 that came back on another head (see [A P1 that comes back on the next head](#a-p1-that-comes-back-on-the-next-head)), the build runs on the strong tier, whatever `escalate_on` lists. Otherwise, after a listed setback (an earlier failed attempt, a blocking P1 finding or a stall) the build is raised to the strong tier; a rework after a round without a P1 stays on the cheaper tier. Otherwise a `model:<tier>` label (for example `model:strong`) sets the tier, and without either the build starts on `tier`. A `model:` value that names no tier, such as a model name, is ignored. The rules look only at the labels, the touched paths, the setback the loop passes and the escalation outcome of the change's latest reviewed head, so a job can never choose its own model. A bad tier, an unknown setback, or sensitive paths or setbacks that are not a list, stop Intakt from starting and name the key.
 
-To see the tier the rules choose, without calling any model, run `php bin/console intakt:build:tier <project> --label=model:strong --path=src/Foo.php --setback=stall` (`--label` and `--path` can be repeated; all options are optional). It prints the tier and why (`default`, `label`, `setback` or `sensitive-path`); an unknown project fails with a message. Starting a build on the chosen tier is not wired yet.
+To see the tier the rules choose, without calling any model, run `php bin/console intakt:build:tier <project> --label=model:strong --path=src/Foo.php --setback=stall --change=my-branch` (`--label` and `--path` can be repeated; all options are optional; `--change` names the branch the build continues). It prints the tier and why (`default`, `label`, `setback`, `confirmed-escalation` or `sensitive-path`); an unknown project fails with a message, and so does a `--change` whose escalation review is still pending (it prints "The escalation review of the change ... is still pending"), because its answer may raise the tier. A first build (`intakt:build:task`) starts on the tier its labels choose; a rework build is not routed yet, so the escalation outcome shows only in these two commands.
 
-To choose the model of a build and record it, run `php bin/console intakt:build:model <project> <task> <attempt> [--label=...] [--path=...] [--setback=...]` (the attempt starts at 1). It applies the same rules, prints the tier, the reason, the provider and the model the project maps that tier to, and writes one `model_chosen` event to the ledger (`php bin/console intakt:ledger:events` shows it). It calls no model. An empty project or task, a project or task that is not valid UTF-8 and an attempt below 1 fail with a message and record nothing. A build runs the Claude Code CLI, so if the project maps the chosen tier to any other provider, the command fails and records nothing. If the ledger cannot record the event, the command fails and the choice does not count. A build never picks its own model: only the rules, your labels, the paths and the earlier round's setback decide.
+To choose the model of a build and record it, run `php bin/console intakt:build:model <project> <task> <attempt> [--label=...] [--path=...] [--setback=...] [--change=...]` (the attempt starts at 1). It applies the same rules, prints the tier, the reason, the provider and the model the project maps that tier to, and writes one `model_chosen` event to the ledger (`php bin/console intakt:ledger:events` shows it). It calls no model. An empty project or task, a project or task that is not valid UTF-8 and an attempt below 1 fail with a message and record nothing. A build runs the Claude Code CLI, so if the project maps the chosen tier to any other provider, the command fails and records nothing. A `--change` whose escalation review is still pending fails and records nothing. If the ledger cannot record the event, the command fails and the choice does not count. A build never picks its own model: only the rules, your labels, the paths, the earlier round's setback and the change's escalation outcome decide.
 
